There's a telnetTiles program, which lets you do exactly that...
mostly.
There's a patch that server admins could run, adds a few lines of code
to add a display type.  With it, when you use telnetTiles, every symbol
gets converted to a tile, and it's a decent emulation of playing with
tiles on your host PC.  Without the patch, telnetTiles does it's best
to *guess* what symbol you're looking at.  It does all the walls, it
does money, a few potions, most of the monsters (although the stronger
'q's all look like mumaks, but by the time you see them, you're either
buff enough that it doesn't matter that your beating on a titanothere,
not a mumak, or either would be scary.)  Whenever a symbol can't be
resolved non-ambiguously, it defaults to the plain symbol.  It also
provides a scrolling message window, mouse click travel, and alt
command mapping, regardless of the way the server is patched.

http://www.geocities.com/jdhilts@sbcglobal.net/

To date, none of the public servers have been interested in adding the
patch.  If enough users send in requests, they may change their mind.
Even without the patch, being able to *see* shades on Orcus' level
makes it worthwhile to me.

